## 5) Safe + reusable queries: parameters and “prepared statement” patterns

If you’re building any real application—especially one that takes user input—**parameterization is not optional**.

Kuzu explicitly calls out that, similar to SQL injection, **Cypher injection** can occur if you dynamically concatenate strings into queries; the recommended practice is to pass parameters instead.

### Parameter syntax

* Use `$name` inside the Cypher statement
* Pass a Python dictionary via the `parameters` argument

✅ Recommended pattern:

```python
min_age = 18
max_age = 30

conn.execute("""
MATCH (p:Person)
WHERE p.age > $min_age AND p.age < $max_age
RETURN p.name;
""", parameters={"min_age": min_age, "max_age": max_age})
```

🚫 Not recommended:

```python
conn.execute(f"""
MATCH (p:Person)
WHERE p.age > {min_age} AND p.age < {max_age}
RETURN p.name;
""")
```

### Why “prepared” matters operationally

Even if you never face an attacker, parameters give you:

* repeatable query templates
* clearer separation between logic and inputs
* simpler testing
* safer DataFrame / external input workflows (see next sections)

